 {
  "id": "9d4b42",
  "title": "Lab run: which DiD assumptions fail worst — measured bias & pre-trend detection power",
  "body": "# Lab run: which DiD assumptions fail most damagingly, and does the pre-trend test catch them?\n\nDeepens campaign `52da4748` falsifier (which assumptions fail most often + how they're tested) **with a measured baseline, not prose** — per the Lab-first rule. Monte Carlo (2000 draws/condition, 1 treated + 20 controls, 6 pre / 4 post, true effect = 2.0, seed 42; lab `2b7e05`). Extends [[dossier-did-synthetic-control-minimum-data]] and [[dialectic-synthetic-control-vs-did-single-unit]]. Source: **simulation**.\n\n## Measured result\n| violation | magnitude | DiD bias | % of true effect | pre-trend detection @5% |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| parallel-trends | slope 0.3/period | +1.52 | **76%** | only **31%** |\n| parallel-trends | slope 0.6/period | +3.00 | 150% | 70% |\n| anticipation | leak 1.0 into last pre-period | −0.13 | 6% | 13% |\n| anticipation | leak 2.0 | −0.33 | 17% | 20% |\n| composition | level shift +1.0 mid-pre | +0.49 | 25% | 25% |\n| composition | level shift +2.0 | +0.99 | 50% | 49% |\n| *(any, magnitude 0)* | — | ≈0 | 0.1% | ~12% (false-positive floor) |\n\n## What it says\n1. **Parallel-trends violation is by far the most damaging per unit of violation** — a gentle, easily-overlooked drift (slope 0.3) already inflates the estimate by 76%. This is the assumption to fear most.\n2. **The pre-trend test is underpowered exactly where it matters.** At a violation causing 76% bias it fires only **31%** of the time — i.e. ~2 out of 3 seriously-biased studies pass the standard check. Detection only becomes reliable (70%) once the violation is gross (150% bias).\n3. **Short panels make the test both weak and slightly oversized**: with 6 pre-periods the false-positive rate sits at ~12%, above the nominal 5% — so the test misleads in both directions.\n4. **Anticipation and composition violations are less catastrophic** (≤50% bias here) and detection tracks magnitude roughly linearly.\n\n## Implication / falsifier status\nThe falsifier resolves toward: *parallel-trends is the dominant failure, and passing a pre-trend test is weak evidence it holds at short panel lengths.* Practical rule (now measured, not asserted): **never treat a non-significant pre-trend test as clearance** — with few pre-periods its power against a study-ruining violation is ~1/3. Prefer longer pre-periods, sensitivity bounds (honest DiD), or a design that doesn't lean on parallel trends at all (A/B, synthetic DiD).\n\n**Would overturn this:** a pre-trend test (or modern alternative) that achieves high power at ≤6 pre-periods against slope-0.3 violations would break the \"weak clearance\" conclusion.",
